#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints a one-time "what to do next" summary at the end of the first chezmoi apply.
# run_once_after_* fires exactly once after source state has been written and other scripts have run.

set -u

cat <<'BANNER'

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                       DOTFILES BOOTSTRAP COMPLETE
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
BANNER

cat <<EOF

What just happened: chezmoi installed shell config, LazyVim, tmux config,
git config, modern CLI tools, and (optionally) the personal/work overlays.

Next steps:

  1. OPEN A FRESH TERMINAL (or run \`exec bash -l\`).
     The current shell pre-dates the new ~/.bashrc and won't pick it up.
{{- if eq .profile "wsl" }}
     New terminals will have starship, ble.sh autosuggestions, eza, etc.

  2. WINDOWS-SIDE FONT (one-time, manual).
     Open Windows Terminal → Settings → Profiles → Ubuntu → Appearance,
     and VSCode → Settings → search "font family". Set both to:
         JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono
     Then restart the terminal. Without this, prompt icons render as boxes.
{{- end }}

  3. CHEAT SHEET.
     Run \`cheat\` (alias in the new shell) for a full reference of
     keybinds, modern CLI swaps, and editing workflow.

  4. FIRST NVIM LAUNCH installs LazyVim plugins (~1-2 min).
     Just run \`nvim\` once and let it finish.

  5. PULL UPDATES later with \`chezmoi update\` (= git pull + apply).

{{- if and .work .bootstrap_work_overlay }}

  Work overlay: cloned to ~/.config/dotfiles-work/. Edit files there
  directly, then commit + push to crumgary/dotfiles-work.
{{- end }}
{{- if .bootstrap_priv_overlay }}

  Personal overlay: cloned to ~/.config/dotfiles-priv/. Edit, commit,
  push to crumgary/dotfiles-priv. Other machines pick up changes on
  \`chezmoi update\`.
{{- end }}

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
EOF
